+## Suspected bugs
+
+1. **CRLF line endings in decor are silently rewritten to LF on display**
+   (pinned by the deterministically failing test
+   `edit::crlf_line_endings_are_preserved`).
+   - **Repro**: `"a = 1\r\n".parse::<DocumentMut>().unwrap().to_string()` returns
+     `"a = 1\n"`. Every `\r` in whitespace/comment/newline decor is dropped, so
+     parsing a Windows-style file and calling `to_string()` rewrites every line
+     ending; editing one value with `toml_edit` rewrites the whole file's EOLs.
+     CRLF inside *string values* is preserved — only decor is affected.
+   - **Code path**: `RawString::encode` / `encode_with_default`
+     (`crates/toml_edit/src/raw_string.rs:88,102`) write raw spans via
+     `for part in raw.split('\r') { … }`, deliberately discarding CRs. Introduced
+     in the span refactor 772f2cf0 (2022) with no comment, test, or changelog
+     entry pinning it as intended.
+   - **Assessment**: the loop is clearly deliberate code, so this may be intended
+     normalization — but it is undocumented and contradicts the crate's central
+     promise ("preserving comments, spaces *and relative order* of items", lib.rs,
+     whose example asserts `doc.to_string() == toml`). No test in the repo asserts
+     either behavior. Recommend raising upstream as a question; test kept failing
+     per instructions not to fix bugs.
+
+2. **Display appends a missing final newline** (excluded from #14's generator,
+   documented here). `"[t]".parse::<DocumentMut>().unwrap().to_string()` returns
+   `"[t]\n"`. A trailing newline at EOF is optional in the grammar, so byte
+   preservation is violated by one byte. Code path: the keyval/table encoders in
+   `crates/toml_edit/src/encode.rs` terminate every construct with `\n`
+   unconditionally. Very likely intended; worth documenting upstream.
+
+3. **Repeated `[[array-of-table]]` headers lose their individual bracket
+   whitespace** (excluded from #14's generator, documented here). Input
+   `"[[k]]\n[[k\t]]\n"` (second header has a tab before `]]`) displays as
+   `"[[k]]\n[[k]]\n"` — the key repr of an array-of-tables is stored once on the
+   parent entry, so later headers are re-rendered with the first occurrence's
+   formatting. A data-model limitation of per-element header decor; low severity
+   but a real preservation gap.
+
+4. **Floats that overflow f64 are parse errors, bounding the valid-text domain**
+   (generator constraint, not asserted as a bug): `1E310` is rejected with
+   "floating-point number overflowed" rather than parsing to `+inf`. The official
+   toml-test compliance suite passes, so this is the maintainers' chosen behavior;
+   the float-text generator caps exponents at two digits with a comment.
+
+5. (Earlier session, `toml_datetime`) `Offset::Custom`'s doc comment
+   (`crates/toml_datetime/src/datetime.rs:171`) says `Minutes: -1_440..1_440`, but
+   `-1440` encodes to `-24:00`, which the parser (correctly, per RFC 3339
+   `time-hour = 00-23`) refuses to re-parse; `±1439` is the real bound. Doc bug;
+   the datetime generators use ±1439 with a comment.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+Nothing was abandoned outright; several generator decisions deliberately narrowed
+domains to *documented* validity rules (constructing valid inputs directly, per the
+skill), each with a code comment:
+
+- **LF-only newlines and always-terminated documents in #14/#17**, and **identical
+  header text across `[[…]]` repetitions**: these exclude the three known
+  preservation deviations above so the sibling properties stay green while the CRLF
+  pin stays failing (skill: exclude known-buggy inputs from siblings, reference the
+  canonical test).
+- **Float exponents ≤ 2 digits** in generated float literals (parser rejects
+  f64 overflow — bug note 4).
+- **Offset minutes ±1439, not the doc's −1440** (bug note 5).
+- **Leap second `60` excluded**: validity depends on a leap-second table the test
+  cannot model. (Seconds-*omitted* times, a TOML 1.1 feature, ARE generated and
+  round-trip fine.)
+- **`nanosecond` only generated when `second` is present**: a fractional part
+  without a seconds part is not representable in the grammar.
+- **NaN compared as NaN↦NaN** in #18's `values_eq`: TOML's `nan` carries no sign or
+  payload, and `Value: PartialEq` is `false` for NaN == NaN; all other floats
+  compare bit-exactly.
+
+One property from the earlier session was *superseded* rather than abandoned: the
+earlier report treated strict byte-identity ("display equals original input") as
+unevidenced because the fuzz target only asserts fixpoint stability. On a closer
+read, lib.rs asserts exactly `doc.to_string() == toml` for its example, so this
+session built the valid-text generator family and asserted byte-identity (#14) —
+which immediately found the three preservation deviations above. The lesson: the
+strongest evidenced property is worth the generator investment.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+1. **Recursive `#[hegel::composite]` does not compile — worth documenting.**
+   Recursive data (TOML values) makes the natural composite draw from itself,
+   failing with an opaque E0283 about `impl Fn(TestCase) -> T: Send`. The escape
+   hatch (plain functions taking `&hegel::TestCase`) works perfectly and both
+   sessions used it heavily; the reference's composite section should mention it.
+   (The current reference.md now documents `compose!`-based recursion; the plain-fn
+   pattern deserves equal billing — it is simpler and was sufficient for a whole
+   ABNF-transcribed generator family.)
+2. **Engine panic on stale failure DB after changing a generator.** After editing a
+   generator whose old counterexample was in `.hegel`, the replay crashed *inside
+   hegeltest* (`run_lifecycle.rs:477: index out of bounds`) rather than reporting a
+   test failure. Gotcha 11's advice (`rm -rf .hegel` when replays make no sense)
+   fixed it, but the failure mode "engine panic ≈ stale DB" could be stated there
+   explicitly — the panic text gives no hint.
+3. **"Generate at the encode-set boundary" and "transcribe the spec as oracle"
+   both paid off exactly as advertised**: the byte-identity property over
+   ABNF-transcribed text found three real preservation deviations (CRLF, EOF
+   newline, AoT header decor) within the first few hundred cases, none of which
+   the crate's own fixpoint-only fuzz target can see.
+4. **"Probe recursion depth directly" guidance validated**: uniform generation
+   never reaches depth 80; the explicit depth sweep pinned the exact recursion
+   boundary (80 ok / 81 err) across five construct kinds and two crates.
+5. **MSRV `cargo add` failure and the ambiguous-package gotcha were both predicted
+   verbatim by the reference** (`--ignore-rust-version`; `-p toml@<version>` because
+   hegeltest itself depends on `toml` 0.9). Zero friction thanks to the docs.
+6. **Keeping a pinned failing test deterministic** (skill's "Keeping a Failing
+   Test" section) was straightforward: the CRLF pin fails on 100% of cases by
+   construction, so it never reads as flaky.
+7. **Minor porting-doc note** (earlier session): an explicit sentence that
+   *strengthening* an existing property (parse-success → round-trip equality) is
+   encouraged when the stronger contract has evidence would help; "one property per
+   test" superficially reads as forbidding it.
